{"name":"Jelly Yoghurt","hex":"#ede6d9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jelly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dessert made by boiling gelatine (or a plant-based alternative such as agar or carrageenan), sugar and some flavouring (often derived from fruit) and allowing it to set.","A clear or translucent fruit preserve, made from fruit juice and set using either naturally occurring, or added, pectin."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make into jelly.","To preserve in jelly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jelly"},{"term":"yoghurt","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of yogurt."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yoghurt"}],"entries":[{"title":"Yogurt","blocks":[{"text":"Yogurt is a food produced by bacterial fermentation of milk. The bacteria ferment the sugars in the milk and produce lactic acid, which acts on milk protein to give yogurt its texture and characteristic tart flavor. Yogurt is most commonly made from cow's milk, but milk from water buffalo, goats, ewes, mares, camels, and yaks can also be used."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Joghurt.jpg/330px-Joghurt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yogurt","wikidata":"Q13317"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Turkish_strained_yogurt.jpg/500px-Turkish_strained_yogurt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Turkish_strained_yogurt.jpg/1280px-Turkish_strained_yogurt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Turkish_strained_yogurt.jpg/500px-Turkish_strained_yogurt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Unstirred Turkish S